  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Mon Jul 13 2026\n\n- Hot and humid through at least Thursday with no rain expected\n\nWe issued a Heat Advisory earlier for the period of Tuesday \nafternoon and evening. Additional heat headlines are very possible \nthrough Thursday.\n\nOur dominating weather feature is a 600 dam 500 mb ridge that is \ncentered just west of the state. This ridge has only strengthened \nits hold as it has built over the area today. 850 mb temps are \naround 19-20C this afternoon. The upper ridge will slowly build \nsouth of the area over the next couple of days. However, the core of \nthe warmest air aloft (+25C) remains to our NW, and moves overhead \nTuesday.\n\nMid 90s F looks likely over most of the forecast area Tuesday, away \nfrom Lake Michigan. In addition, higher dew points are also moving \nin from the WNW, with upper 60s to near 70 expected. That will put \nheat indices in the lower 100s.\n\nThe upper ridge axis and core of the warmest temps start to slip \nsouth of the area by Wednesday. However, with better mixing with the \nhigh moving away will likely keep highs in the mid 90s on Wednesday. \nThe trend will continue with the ridge weakening, and 850 mb temps \nslowly cooling.\n\nWe feel it is likely that Heat Advisory criteria (100F) will be met \nTuesday and Wednesday, and maybe just a tad shy on Thursday as a \nweak front slips through with the lowering heights. Coordination \nwith other offices brought a plan to deal with headlines daily, vs \nover the entire period. That is due to some signal in the models of \ntrying to mix down much drier air. This would limit the heat indices \na tad. \n\n- Rain chances start Thursday night with cooler temps filtering in \n\nThat front that moves through on Thursday is quite weak, with very \nlittle convergence along it. However, it will gradually become a \npotential boundary to focus convection along it with the cap \nweakening with the lower heights settling in. Models are indicating \nthat the first chances of rain may arrive as early as Thursday night \nwith a weak wave embedded in the flow could act on the gradient of \nbetter instability.\n\nMore clouds, lower heights, and cooler temps aloft will all lead to \nsfc temps slowly trending cooler through the weekend. We do not see \nhigh precipitation chances for the first half of the weekend. There \nis not really a well organized upper wave/sfc low until later \nSunday. At that time, rain chances will be the best for the seven \nday period.",
